---
// The About page. The essay and its asides are Meredith's words, kept exactly.
// The Ember easter egg (EmberClick) stays on this page by her choice.
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import EmberClick from '../components/EmberClick.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
import '../styles/pages.css';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={`About | ${SITE_TITLE}`} description="The story behind the work. Meredith McGee writes about how she got from Rochester to Yale to Boston, and why public health keeps pulling her in." />
		<noscript><style>.aside-note[hidden] { display: block !important; }</style></noscript>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main id="main">
			<header class="phero p-plain">
				<div class="wrap phero-in">
					<div class="phero-text">
						<p class="label">About</p>
						<h1 class="phero-h">Hi, I’m Meredith</h1>
						<p class="lede">Researcher, writer, and builder.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="phero-side">
						<img alt="Meredith McGee" height="801" src="/img/meredith.jpg" width="800"/>
					</div>
				</div>
			</header>
			<div class="essay">
				<section class="chap-band">
					<div class="wrap chapter">
						<h2 class="chap-h" style="--r:1"><span>01</span> Origins</h2>
						<p style="--r:2">I grew up in Rochester, New York. In elementary school, I spent my afternoons after school sitting at a desk against the back wall of my dad's office, watching him run his own business, try new ideas, and figure out what to do when they didn't work. I also saw how hard it is to keep a small family business going. Watching him work that hard is where my passion for small business started, and small businesses and the people who run them are still at the center of my work.</p>
						<p style="--r:3">By high school, I was doing my own version of what I'd watched him do, and I figured out that I learn best by going all in. Most of what I loved looked unrelated on the surface: stage managing theater productions, training for track and field, spending my free time on whatever corner of history I couldn't stop thinking about. But each one was teaching me to see how things connect, how every part of a system shapes the others, whether that's how foot positioning in the ring can change the trajectory of an entire throw, or how science, technology, and history came together to finally tell the story of what happened to the Romanovs. I've never been able to pick just one thing, which is how I ended up at Lafayette studying both biology and history.</p>
						<p style="--r:4">Earning a B.S. in biology and a B.A. in history meant I got the "why don't you just pick one?" question a lot. But to me, both were asking the same question in different languages: how do complex systems work, and what happens to the people inside them? I was a Division I track and field athlete throwing the hammer until I had to step away for medical reasons. So I channeled what I learned from my dad and pivoted, putting all that energy into building what my campus was missing.</p>
						<figure class="photo rv" style="--r:4">
							<img alt="Lavender Lane house at Lafayette College with rainbow chalk walkway" height="1014" loading="lazy" src="/img/lavender-lane.jpg" width="760"/>
							<figcaption>Lavender Lane, the day we chalked the walkway.</figcaption>
						</figure>
						<p style="--r:5">My partner K and I co-founded OUT Lafayette to create space for LGBTQ+ students to gather, and we launched <button aria-controls="aside-2" aria-expanded="false" class="fn" type="button">Lavender Lane</button>, a living-learning community in a converted sorority house that became the college's first gender-affirming and gender-neutral housing option. This culminated in the project I'm still proudest of: bringing panels of the National AIDS Memorial Quilt to campus for the first time in three decades.<span class="aside-note" hidden id="aside-2">We converted a former sorority house into gender-neutral housing, which sounds simple until you realize how many institutional hurdles a sorority house has. It became a real home. I still think about the students who needed it before it existed.</span></p>
						<p style="--r:6">I also got <span class="ember-trigger" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Click to meet Ember">Ember</span> during college, a black lab who has been my best friend ever since.</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section class="chap-band">
					<div class="wrap chapter">
						<h2 class="chap-h" style="--r:1"><span>02</span> Yale</h2>
						<p style="--r:2">After Lafayette, I went to the Yale School of Public Health for my MPH in Social and Behavioral Sciences. Yale gave me the formal training to match instincts I'd been acting on for years. I worked as a graduate research assistant at the LGBTQ+ Mental Health Initiative, where I contributed to an affirmative cognitive behavioral therapy trial and used CDC survey data from multiple states to analyze how cannabis use patterns differ by sexual identity. I also designed and led an evaluation of how students experienced their public health internships, turning what I heard into recommendations that changed how the program prepared students for the field.</p>
						<p style="--r:3">Yale was also where I started doing policy work. I completed an analysis for the U.S. Senate Finance Committee examining outcomes for youth in congregate care settings, and worked on a scoping review of Housing First outcomes. Those projects taught me something I keep coming back to: the distance between how a system is supposed to work on paper and how people actually experience it is where some of the most important questions live.</p>
						<figure class="photo rv" style="--r:3">
							<img alt="Meredith presenting cannabis research at Yale School of Public Health" height="639" loading="lazy" src="/img/yale-poster.jpg" width="900"/>
							<figcaption>APHA 2024. Diet Weed or Deception?</figcaption>
						</figure>
						<p style="--r:4">I've been with Bradbury-Sullivan LGBT Community Center in Pennsylvania since college, when I started there as an intern. I stayed on through Yale, and I'm still their grant specialist today. I was there through a hard stretch, when the center lost much of its funding and staff. We rebuilt, and in 2025 and 2026, while nonprofit fundraising was down almost everywhere, we had some of our strongest years. That work taught me that development is public health work. I blend program data, numbers, and stories from staff and community members, so a funder sees both the scope of the need and the people behind it. I've helped secure <button aria-controls="aside-3" aria-expanded="false" class="fn" type="button">over a million dollars</button> for health equity and community programs. Getting my MPH while writing grants changed how I do both, and it shaped <a href="/how-i-work">how I work</a> today.<span class="aside-note" hidden id="aside-3">That figure comes from event sponsorships and in-kind support, foundation and corporate grants, donor-advised funds, community foundation grants, and a few federal and state grants.</span></p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section class="pullband">
					<div class="wrap">
						<p class="pullband-q rv">The distance between how a system is supposed to work on paper and how people actually experience it is where some of the most important questions live.</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section class="chap-band">
					<div class="wrap chapter">
						<h2 class="chap-h" style="--r:1"><span>03</span> Now</h2>
						<figure class="photo rv" style="--r:2">
							<img alt="Meredith with colleagues in Boston" height="573" loading="lazy" src="/img/boston-group.jpg" width="1000"/>
							<figcaption>Leadership training and crash course with the Parabola team and attendees.</figcaption>
						</figure>
						<p style="--r:2">These days, I'm in Boston. I'm a Public Health Researcher at Parabola Center for Law and Policy, where I study how cannabis legalization shapes public health. With Faith English, I co-led a study built on 37 interviews with cannabis consumers across nine states, asking how legal markets are working for the people who use them. Parabola's work now reaches into tech too, like our model AI policy for schools, but the question stays the same: who new markets are built for, and whether small businesses and communities get squeezed out by the biggest players.</p>
						<figure class="photo rv" style="--r:4;--span:3">
							<img alt="Meredith with Rachel Levine and colleagues at the Pennsylvania LGBTQIA+ Health Summit" height="1021" loading="lazy" src="/img/rachel-levine.jpg" width="760"/>
							<figcaption>With Rachel Levine at the Pennsylvania LGBTQIA+ Health Summit.</figcaption>
						</figure>
						<p style="--r:3">I also write <a href="https://groundlevelmm.substack.com" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Ground Level</a>, a Substack about how systems shape lived experience, from cannabis to housing to the cost of care.</p>
						<p style="--r:4">While I was at Yale, I spent two years in a New Haven rental fighting for basic repairs. When my property manager went quiet, I dug through public records until I found the person who owned the building, several layers removed from anyone I'd ever spoken to, and I got my repairs. But I couldn't stop thinking about everyone without the time or the stubbornness to trace an LLC back to a name. So I built what I wished had existed. <a href="https://ratemyplace.org" rel="noopener" target="_blank">RateMyPlace</a> is a tenant review platform grounded in public health research, built so that what one renter learns about a building doesn't vanish when they move out.</p>
						<p style="--r:5">K, <span class="ember-trigger" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Click to meet Ember">Ember</span>, and I live in Brighton. Outside of work, I'm learning to cook and spending as much time outside with Ember as I can.</p>
						<p style="--r:6">All of this is what I bring to the work I do with clients now. I help small nonprofits, research teams, and social entrepreneurs with funding, research, and writing, the same mix I've been blending since that desk in my dad's office. If you're doing good work and need help showing it, I'd love to hear from you.</p>
					</div>
				</section>
			</div>
			<section class="wrap sec">
				<p class="cta"><a class="btn solid" href="/work-with-me">Work with me</a> <a class="textlink" href="/how-i-work">How I work</a> <a class="textlink" href="/contact">Say hello</a></p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<EmberClick />
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<script>
	// The dotted phrases in the essay open a short aside under their paragraph.
	import { onPage } from '../scripts/page';

	onPage((signal) => {
		document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.fn').forEach((button) => {
			const note = document.getElementById(button.getAttribute('aria-controls') || '');
			if (!note) return;
			button.addEventListener(
				'click',
				() => {
					const open = button.getAttribute('aria-expanded') !== 'true';
					button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
					note.hidden = !open;
				},
				{ signal },
			);
		});
	});
</script>
